WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.126 --> 00:00:02.126
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Can I
get some milk and sugar

00:00:02.126 --> 00:00:03.820
with my coffee please?

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:05.345
SPEAKER: [SPEAKING SPANISH]

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.610
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Now that
is some awesome coffee.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.680
Translation is fun, isn't it?

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.830
Well, stay with me as we explore
custom translation models

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:18.360
to power global apps.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.760
Welcome to "AI Adventures" where
we explore the art, science,

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.159
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:32.159 --> 00:00:34.380
I'm Priyanka
Vergadia, and today we

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:37.230
are talking about custom
translation models.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:39.780
I was in Peru last
year, which is mostly

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.640
a Spanish-speaking country.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.160
I do not speak Spanish,
so I had to rely

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.940
on Google Translate for even
minor things such as can I

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.950
get milk in my coffee, please?

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:54.210
Now imagine if I was to go live
and work in Peru for a year,

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:57.510
helping developers implement
machine learning and AI maybe.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.960
The machine-learning field has
unique terms and references

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.319
to mathematical concepts
which might not be captured

00:01:04.319 --> 00:01:05.640
in a standard dictionary.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:09.540
For a custom use case like this,
the standard Translation API

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.200
may not be sufficient.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:12.840
To provide the
best experience, I

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.900
would need to make sure things
don't get lost in translation,

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:19.830
and that would need a custom
model specific to the ML and AI

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.350
terminologies to
communicate effectively

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:23.940
with the Spanish-speaking
developers.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.820
This is probably true
in your field as well.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:30.300
That's where AutoML
Translation comes to rescue.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:34.080
AutoML Translation lets
you create your own custom

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.330
translation models
so that translation

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:39.990
queries return results
specific to your domain.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:43.080
To understand this further,
let's take another example.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.510
Imagine you run a
financial-reporting purpose

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:48.750
that has an opportunity
to expand to Peru, India,

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.070
and France.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:53.490
Those markets require that
your time-sensitive financial

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:56.130
documents are
translated in real time.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:59.580
AutoML Translation can help you
automate the translation job

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:02.760
for over a hundred language
pairs in a scalable way,

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.750
allowing you to enter those
newer markets quickly.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.120
Now, you might be asking
yourself, when would

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.090
I use AutoML over
the Translation API?

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.510
Translation API does a great
job with general-purpose text,

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:17.620
and with advanced
glossary features

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:20.670
you can take it further by
providing a dictionary of words

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:21.630
or phrases.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:25.320
I covered this in detail in the
previous episode linked below.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:27.900
But there are times when
you don't have a glossary

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.450
or know exactly which
word translations

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.010
you want to control.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:35.130
AutoML Translation
shines in those cases

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:38.130
in that last mile between
generic translation

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:41.370
tasks and specific
niche vocabularies.

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:43.860
What's amazing is
AutoML's custom model

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:46.860
is built on top of the
generic Translation API

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:50.310
model for domain-specific
content that matters to you.

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:53.250
Now let's say you've established
that you need a custom

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:54.540
translation model.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:55.860
How would you create one?

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.350
Well, we will just prepare
and source the data

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:00.880
and feed it into
AutoML Translation.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.660
It then trains, deploys,
and serves the model,

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.790
and provides us with
an easy interface

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.650
to evaluate and test it.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:11.370
It creates an easy-to-use
REST API to make predictions.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:14.100
High-quality data is
critical ingredient

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.340
in creating an effective
AutoML Translation model.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:20.550
As a general best practice,
make sure your sentence pairs

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:24.750
do the best-possible job of
covering the vocabulary, usage,

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:29.400
and grammatical quirks of your
industry or the area of focus.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.260
For more details,
I've included a link

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:33.810
in the description for
some best practices of how

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:36.640
to assess and source your data.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:40.290
Once we have the data, we head
over to the AutoML Translation

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:44.040
UI and create the data set by
selecting the source and target

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:45.120
language.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.920
Upload the files
from your computer,

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:50.340
and select the folder in
Cloud Storage for these files

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:51.840
to be stored.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.930
Then successfully uploaded,
we can review our data set

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:56.760
and the sentence pairs.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:00.460
With the data all set, we
are ready to train the model.

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:03.210
But this step can take
several hours, depending

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:04.660
on the amount of data.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:07.460
So let's grab coffee
in the meantime.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:09.980
All right, it looks like
our training is complete,

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:12.800
and right here it is showing
us the high-level metrics

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:16.910
for our model and its BLEU
or Bilingual Evaluation

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:20.510
Understudies score, which takes
a ground-truth translation

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:23.510
and compares it to a
machine translation.

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:26.720
The more overlap between the
human and machine output,

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:29.030
the higher the BLEU score.

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:30.980
You can see the score
for Google's base

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:33.590
model and your custom
model as well as

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:35.580
the difference between them.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:39.020
In this case, we see a boost
of a few points over the base

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:40.360
model.

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:42.860
Now the moment of truth.

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:46.400
Let's head over to our
Predict tab to test our model.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:49.660
We can even compare the
results from our custom model

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:53.260
to the Google NMT, which is
the Neural Machine Translation

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:55.630
model, to see the difference.

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:58.750
In this episode, we learned
how AutoML Translation

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:02.050
helps you create high-quality,
production-ready, custom

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:04.510
translation models quickly.

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:06.790
How are you using the
AutoML Translation

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:08.770
model in your use cases?

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:11.180
Share with me in
the comments below.

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:13.780
Thank you for watching
this episode of "Cloud AI

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:14.470
Adventures."

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:16.930
And if you enjoyed it,
click that Like button,

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:20.170
and be sure to subscribe to get
all the latest episodes right

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:21.100
when they come up.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:23.140
I look forward to
seeing you next time

